"use strict";(self.webpackChunk_N_E=self.webpackChunk_N_E||[]).push([[404],{404:e=>{e.exports=JSON.parse('{"id":"base-05","slug":"base-05","trackId":"base","order":6,"title":"Stablecoins: USDT, USDC and DAI","subtitle":"Clear Rules for Protecting Capital","estimatedMinutes":10,"access":"free","quizCount":3,"blueprintCount":0,"trainerIds":["HUB-CALC-04"],"markdown":"Lesson 5 \xb7 7 min\\n\\n## Stablecoins\\n\\nWhat USDT, USDC and DAI Are\\n\\nPrevious articles explained that cryptocurrency can be held in a wallet and transferred through different networks. An obvious question follows:\\n\\nif Bitcoin, Ethereum ↗ (opens in a new tab) and other cryptocurrencies constantly change in price, how can you use crypto simply to store or transfer money? Imagine holding cryptocurrency worth $1 000 today. Tomorrow, its price falls by 10%, leaving you with $900.\\n\\nSuch fluctuations are familiar in investing, but inconvenient when you only want to preserve money, receive payment or transfer funds. Stablecoins were created to address that problem.\\n\\n### A Stablecoin Is a “Digital Dollar”\\n\\nAt its simplest, a stablecoin is a digital token designed to maintain a stable price. The most popular stablecoins are pegged to the US dollar. The idea is straightforward:\\n\\n1 USDT ≈ $1\\n\\n1 USDC ≈ $1\\n\\n1 DAI ≈ $1\\n\\nTherefore, 1 000 USDT should be worth approximately $1 000. This is why stablecoins are often called digital dollars. But remember:\\n\\nUSDT and USDC are not dollars in a bank account. They are separate digital assets whose prices aim to remain near one dollar.\\n\\n### Why Stablecoins Are Useful\\n\\nFor someone living with a stable currency and unrestricted dollar access, this question may seem unusual. Many people do not have those conditions. Foreign currency can be difficult to buy, subject to controls, or held against a rapidly depreciating national currency.\\n\\nSometimes someone nominally owns dollars in a bank account but cannot freely withdraw or transfer them. Russia is one example. Restrictions on withdrawing foreign-currency cash have applied since 2022 and were extended by the Bank of Russia until 9 March 2027.\\n\\nFunds credited to foreign-currency accounts after 9 September 2022 cannot be withdrawn as cash dollars or euros; payment is made in rubles at the bank’s rate on the withdrawal date. A banking app showing:\\n\\n$10 000\\n\\ndoes not necessarily mean you can visit the bank and collect $10 000 in cash dollars. While money remains in a bank, access depends on more than you. Bank rules and government requirements apply.\\n\\nSo do currency controls, sanctions and international financial infrastructure. Stablecoins offer another way to hold dollar-linked value. If USDT or USDC is in your own wallet, you control access and can send it to someone else yourself.\\n\\nFor some, that is convenient. For others, it is among the few available ways to hold dollar value and transfer it across borders. Self-custody does not mean stablecoins have no restrictions, however. We examine some below.\\n\\n### How a Stablecoin Maintains a Price Near $1\\n\\nWhy should a digital token be worth one dollar at all? Different stablecoins use different mechanisms. A simple analogy is a storage token. You hand over $100.\\n\\nIn return, 100 digital tokens are issued. Each should correspond to roughly one dollar of value. As long as sufficient backing exists and the market trusts the system, the token price remains near $1. Reserves do not necessarily consist of cash dollars. They can include bank deposits, government bonds and other financial assets.\\n\\nThis broadly describes the two best-known stablecoins, USDT and USDC.\\n\\nTap to zoom in\\n\\n#### USDT\\n\\nTether ↗ (opens in a new tab) issues USDT and holds reserves intended to back the tokens in circulation. The basic logic is:\\n\\neach $1 of circulating USDT should have corresponding backing. The market therefore expects:\\n\\n1 USDT ≈ $1\\n\\nUSDT is widely used for transfers, trading and holding dollar-linked value. For many people, it is their first experience with cryptocurrency.\\n\\n#### USDC\\n\\nUSDC works similarly. It is issued by Circle ↗ (opens in a new tab), backed by reserves and designed to remain near one dollar. A simple beginner’s comparison is:\\n\\nUSDT — Tether’s digital dollar.\\n\\nUSDC — Circle’s digital dollar.\\n\\nThey address similar needs, but different companies issue them.\\n\\n#### DAI Works Somewhat Differently\\n\\nDAI also aims to remain near one dollar, but its model differs from the simple formula:\\n\\n“A company holds dollars and issues tokens.” DAI operates within the Sky system, formerly MakerDAO ↗ (opens in a new tab), whose backing can include cryptocurrency and assets linked to traditional finance. In the simplest description, users deposit crypto such as ETH as collateral and borrow DAI. You do not need DAI’s detailed mechanics yet.\\n\\nThe project has existed for a long time and generally operates stably. DAI has nevertheless lost its peg: in March 2023, it fell to about $0,88 because USDC accounted for a significant share of its collateral.\\n\\n### Why the Price Is Not Always Exactly $1\\n\\nA stablecoin need not cost exactly:\\n\\n$1,000000 every second. The market may show:\\n\\n$0,999 or $1,001. Small deviations are normal.\\n\\nA problem begins when the price moves far from one dollar and stays there. For example:\\n\\n1 USDT = $0,80\\n\\nThis indicates a problem and doubts about the token’s ability to preserve value. It is called a dollar depeg. Even the largest stablecoins have experienced it. In March 2023, Silicon Valley Bank failed in the United States. Circle ↗ (opens in a new tab) held $3,3 billion of USDC reserves there.\\n\\nUSDC fell to about $0,87 over the weekend and returned to $1 only after US authorities guaranteed repayment of the bank’s deposits.\\n\\nWhat Are Your 1 000 Stablecoins Worth? Token price $1,000\\n\\n$1 000. Ordinary fluctuation: $0,999 or $1,001 — this is expected.\\n\\nUSDT and USDC can be frozen: their issuing companies can block specific addresses. DAI cannot be frozen.\\n\\n### Can Your Stablecoins Be Frozen?\\n\\nYes, some stablecoins include that capability. Tether ↗ (opens in a new tab) and Circle ↗ (opens in a new tab), for example, can block certain addresses holding USDT and USDC. A mechanism in the tokens lets the company prohibit operations from a particular address.\\n\\nIf an address is blocked, the affected tokens cannot freely be transferred to another wallet. Other cryptocurrencies at the same address are not necessarily affected.\\n\\nImportant: the entire wallet is not frozen; movement of a particular stablecoin is blocked.\\n\\nA distinctive feature of DAI is that it cannot be frozen.\\n\\n#### Why Does This Capability Exist?\\n\\nThe main reason is legal compliance and preventing unlawful financial activity. Addresses can be blocked in connection with:\\n- fraud and cryptocurrency theft;\\n- money laundering;\\n- financing illegal activity;\\n- sanctioned individuals or organizations;\\n- law-enforcement investigations.\\n\\nThis does not mean every USDT or USDC user must constantly fear a freeze, but you need to understand that the mechanism exists.\\n\\n#### How Can You Avoid a Freeze?\\n\\nChoose carefully which platforms you interact with. Before sending money to an exchange, conversion service or another crypto service, investigate:\\n- How well known and reliable is it?\\n- How long has it operated?\\n- Are its rules and service terms clear?\\n- Is it subject to sanctions?\\n- Are there numerous complaints about freezes or withdrawal problems?\\n\\nAvoid sanctioned platforms and addresses associated with illegal activity.\\n\\nBe particularly cautious with unlicensed conversion services, dubious offers and transfers from strangers. Check both the rate offered and the service’s legality.\\n\\nYou do not need to fear the freezing mechanism itself. Understand how it works and choose crypto platforms responsibly.\\n\\n### Stablecoins Give More Control Without Removing Risk\\n\\nHolding stablecoins in your own wallet gives you direct control over fund access. It does not make them fully independent or risk-free. Their risks include:\\n- the token issuer;\\n- the quality and availability of backing;\\n- the ability to freeze individual addresses in some stablecoins;\\n- a dollar depeg;\\n- interaction with unreliable venues.\\n\\n“Stable” does not mean “risk-free.” Until you have experience, the source recommends staying within USDT and USDC rather than other stablecoins. These established companies underpin the industry.\\n\\n### The Essentials\\n\\nIf you forget everything else, remember:\\n\\nBitcoin and ETH can fluctuate sharply. Stablecoins aim to maintain a value near $1. Tether ↗ (opens in a new tab) issues USDT. Circle ↗ (opens in a new tab) issues USDC.\\n\\nDAI uses a different collateral system. USDT and USDC issuers can freeze tokens at particular addresses. DAI has no equivalent freezing function. Stablecoins let you store and transfer dollar-linked value through your own cryptocurrency wallet, but this freedom carries risks.\\n\\nUnderstand not only how to use a stablecoin, but who issues it, what maintains its price and which restrictions may exist.\\n\\nChoose reliable venues, follow security rules and avoid services whose origin or reputation is doubtful.\\n\\n### Check Yourself 1 / 3\\n\\nWhat was the primary purpose of dollar stablecoins?\\n\\n### Terms Used in This Lesson:\\n- **Bond** — A debt security. You are a lender, not a shareholder: you lend money for a fixed interest payment (coupon), and the principal is repaid at maturity. Lower risk than shares\\n- **Stablecoin** — A cryptocurrency pegged one-to-one to the dollar (USDT, USDC). A “dollar flash drive”: transfer it to another country in a minute. More money passes through stablecoins than through Bitcoin\\n- **Issuer** — The entity issuing a security or currency: a company, a government or, for a stablecoin, a private company. The security’s reliability depends on the issuer’s reliability\\n- **DAI** — A stablecoin backed not by dollars but by an excess of other crypto collateral in a smart contract. A more “honest” decentralized option, but also a more complex one\\n- **Ethereum** — Not a currency but a platform, a “world computer,” running smart contracts, DeFi and most stablecoins. Second-largest in size and trust\\n- **USDC** — Circle’s stablecoin, considered the most transparent: regular audits and clearly described reserves\\n- **USDT (Tether)** — The largest stablecoin, supporting almost all crypto turnover. For Russians after 2022, a practical tool for cross-border transfers outside banks\\n- **Token** — A digital asset that exists within a network, such as USDT or USDC. The same token can exist on different networks, and those are different versions of it\\n- **Depeg** — A stablecoin moving away from its $1 peg for an extended period. USDC fell to approximately $0,87 in March 2023\\n\\n### End-of-Lesson Quiz (“Check Yourself”):\\n**Question 1: What was the primary purpose of dollar stablecoins?**\\n- ⚪ Option 1: To make their price rise continuously\\n- ✅ **[CORRECT ANSWER]** Option 2: To store and transfer value close to the dollar\\n- ⚪ Option 3: To replace Bitcoin completely\\n\\n**Question 2: What does it mean if 1 USDT trades around $0,999 or $1,001 for a while?**\\n- ⚪ Option 1: The stablecoin has definitely failed\\n- ✅ **[CORRECT ANSWER]** Option 2: Small market deviations from $1 can be normal\\n- ⚪ Option 3: USDT is no longer backed\\n\\n**Question 3: A stablecoin is called “stable.” Does that mean it has no risks?**\\n- ⚪ Option 1: Yes, if it is dollar-pegged\\n- ✅ **[CORRECT ANSWER]** Option 2: No. Collateral, issuer, individual-address freeze and depeg risks remain\\n- ⚪ Option 3: Only Bitcoin and ETH have risks\\n\\n\\n---","blueprints":[],"quiz":[{"id":"q-base-05-1","prompt":"What was the primary purpose of dollar stablecoins?","options":[{"id":"base-05-1-1","label":"To make their price rise continuously","isCorrect":false,"feedback":"Incorrect. To store and transfer value close to the dollar"},{"id":"base-05-1-2","label":"To store and transfer value close to the dollar","isCorrect":true,"feedback":"Correct. To store and transfer value close to the dollar"},{"id":"base-05-1-3","label":"To replace Bitcoin completely","isCorrect":false,"feedback":"Incorrect. To store and transfer value close to the dollar"}],"explanation":"To store and transfer value close to the dollar","sourceRef":"CAPITAL_BASE:1044–1047 (Verbatim)"},{"id":"q-base-05-2","prompt":"What does it mean if 1 USDT trades around $0,999 or $1,001 for a while?","options":[{"id":"base-05-2-1","label":"The stablecoin has definitely failed","isCorrect":false,"feedback":"Incorrect. Small market deviations from $1 can be normal"},{"id":"base-05-2-2","label":"Small market deviations from $1 can be normal","isCorrect":true,"feedback":"Correct. Small market deviations from $1 can be normal"},{"id":"base-05-2-3","label":"USDT is no longer backed","isCorrect":false,"feedback":"Incorrect. Small market deviations from $1 can be normal"}],"explanation":"Small market deviations from $1 can be normal","sourceRef":"CAPITAL_BASE:1049–1052 (Verbatim)"},{"id":"q-base-05-3","prompt":"A stablecoin is called “stable.” Does that mean it has no risks?","options":[{"id":"base-05-3-1","label":"Yes, if it is dollar-pegged","isCorrect":false,"feedback":"Incorrect. No. Collateral, issuer, individual-address freeze and depeg risks remain"},{"id":"base-05-3-2","label":"No. Collateral, issuer, individual-address freeze and depeg risks remain","isCorrect":true,"feedback":"Correct. No. Collateral, issuer, individual-address freeze and depeg risks remain"},{"id":"base-05-3-3","label":"Only Bitcoin and ETH have risks","isCorrect":false,"feedback":"Incorrect. No. Collateral, issuer, individual-address freeze and depeg risks remain"}],"explanation":"No. Collateral, issuer, individual-address freeze and depeg risks remain","sourceRef":"CAPITAL_BASE:1054–1057 (Verbatim)"}],"source":{"file":"00_FREE_GIFT_MakeCash_Crypto_And_Capital_Base_15_Lessons.md","sha256":"88a3efb162434bb8a9b3b000caee579d9c27005a8ea46ddf4ef9cafb50dc45cc","lineStart":858,"lineEnd":1062}}')}}]);